        "text": "\u2022 80 : HTTP\n\u2022 443 : HTTP Secure ( HTTPS )\nPort numbers beyond 1024 are for general use and are also known as ephemeral ports .\nA port is always associated with an IP address. Ultimately, a socket is a combination of an IP address\nand a port. For more information on network sockets, you can refer to RFC 147 ( https://tools.\nietf.org/html/rfc147 ). For well-known ports, see RFC 1340 ( https://tools.ietf.\norg/html/rfc1340 ).\nNow, let\u2019s put the knowledge we\u2019ve gained so far to work by looking at how to configure the local\nnetworking stack in Linux.",
